            <ArticleTitle>Relationship between body mass index and fibromyalgia features.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>27-31</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">to evaluate the relationship between body mass index (BMI) and features of the fibromyalgia syndrome (FMS).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">211 female patients with FMS seen consecutively in our rheumatology clinic were analyzed. Spearman correlation was used. Further, FMS features were compared at different levels of BMI (kg/m2), e.g., &lt; 25.00 vs &gt; or = 25.00 (normal vs overweight). P value of &lt; or = 0.01 was accepted as significant.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">A significant positive correlation was found between BMI and age (p&lt;0.001) and a negative correlation between BMI and education (p&lt;0.009). Health Assessment Questionnaire (HAQ) score was significantly correlated with BMI (p&lt;0.001), whereas fatigue and number of tender points (TP) showed a trend (p=0.035 and 0.037, respectively).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The HAQ score is significantly associated with BMI in FMS with a trend towards significance for fatigue and TP. Weight loss may improve physical functioning in this disorder.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
